 "literal": "Not going, owner if stays, land having sulked, like wife will feign anger.",
 "translation": "If the owner stays away without visiting his field, the land will sulk and feign anger like a neglected wife.",
 "modern": "நிலத்திற்கு உரியவன் அங்குச் செல்லாமல் வீட்டிலேயே தங்கியிருந்தால், அவனுடைய மனைவியைப் போல அந்த நிலமும் அவனிடம் வெறுப்புற்றுப் பிணங்கிவிடும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet personifies agricultural land as a wife. If the farmer, who is the owner ('கிழவன்'), does not visit his field and stays away, the land will feel neglected. Just as a wife would sulk and feign anger ('ஊடி') when ignored by her husband, the land will sulk ('புலந்து') and refuse to yield crops. The poet beautifully applies the dynamics of a romantic relationship to the bond between a farmer and his land, emphasizing that constant attention is required for a good harvest.",
